WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Welcome to Web Programming, the companion podcast for COMPSCI 326 at UMass Amherst.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.600
I'm Tim Richards.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.280
This episode goes with Unit 10, CRUD Against a File.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.480
It is meant for a walk, a bus ride, or the dishes.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.520
It does not replace reading the unit, and there is no code you need to see to follow

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.800
along.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.080
When I talk about code, I will say what it does rather than read it out character by

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.080
character.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.680
Here is where we are.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.640
Your entries app can already do three of the four things a data backed app needs to

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.640
do.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.640
It can create an entry with a post, it can read the list with a get, and it can delete

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.840
an entry with a delete.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.320
What it cannot do is change an entry once it exists.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:55.320
If you get a title wrong right now, the only fix is to delete the entry and add it again.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.120
That loses its place in the list, and it is not something any real app asks a person

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.520
to do.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.840
This unit adds the missing piece, which is Update.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:11.840
By the end, you have a complete app that can create, read, update, and delete, still stored

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.440
in a plain JSON file.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.760
Strip away the screens, and almost every app is a list of records that people make, look

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.440
at, change, and throw away.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.080
A contact list.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.280
A to-do list.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.560
A shopping cart.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.080
A class roster.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.280
A set of blog posts.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.000
Each one is a collection of things, and the person using it wants to do the same four

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.440
things to each thing.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.720
Those four are create, read, update, and delete, and the first letter spell crud.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.240
The name is worth having because it lets you check an app for gaps in a few seconds.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.600
For every kind of record in your project, ask four questions.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.680
Can I make one?

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.720
Can I see one?

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.720
Can I change one?

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.760
Can I remove one?

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.760
Your Notes app answered yes to three of those before this unit.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.480
The fourth is the one people notice first when it is missing, because fixing a mistake

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:11.080
is the most ordinary thing anyone does with something they have written.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.720
The four operations are older than the web.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.440
They are what every database has always offered.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:25.000
The database language SQL from the 1970s spells them insert, select, update, and delete.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.360
The acronym crud is usually credited to James Martin's 1983 book on database design, and

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.200
it's spread because it gave programmers one word for the plain core of a program that

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.040
handles data.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.000
HTTP picked up the same four ideas as methods.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.440
Get and post came first.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.640
Rout and delete were defined in HTTP version 1.1, whose first specification appeared in

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.400
January 1997.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.160
Around the year 2000, Roy Fielding's dissertation described REST, a way of designing web services

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.880
where every record has its own URL, and the method says what to do to it.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.400
That is exactly the shape of the route you are about to write.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.440
The URL names one entry, entry slash three, for example.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:20.600
The method says what happens to it, get reads it, put replaces it, delete removes it.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:26.200
Once you see that pattern, you can guess most of an unfamiliar API just from its list of

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.920
URLs.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.080
So what does the new route look like?

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.080
It is a fourth route in your entries router matched to the same ID pattern your delete

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.840
route already uses.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.520
And it does the same four steps as every other route in that file.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:49.120
Read the file, parse it into an array, do one thing to the array, write the array back.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.720
The one thing this time is replacement.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.600
Create pushes a new entry onto the end of the array, delete splices one out, update

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.160
assigns a new value to a position, so whatever sat there is replaced, that really is the

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.480
whole difference.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.600
There are two checks before the replacement happens.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.440
First, is there an entry at that ID at all?

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.600
If not, the server answers with a 404, not found.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.400
Second, are the new values any good?

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:25.160
For that you reuse the validate entry function from unit 09 exactly as written.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:31.080
If the title or body is missing, the server answers with a 400, bad request, and the same

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.280
message the create form already shows.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.280
Notice that you did not write any new validation code.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:43.720
The function that guards create now guards update to because both are really asking the

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.240
same question.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.560
Is this a well formed entry?

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.040
That is the payoff of pulling validation into one function back in unit 09, a fourth

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.640
route and zero new checks.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.780
And like every route in the file, the new one goes through the async handler wrapper.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:08.960
If something goes wrong inside it, say the JSON file no longer parses or the right fails,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.080
the error reaches your error handling middleware and comes back as a 500.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.280
The server keeps running instead of crashing.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.500
On the page, editing needs something the user did not have before, a form for an entry

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.420
that already exists.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:30.240
Rather than sending them to a whole new page, the app swaps the entries text for a small

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.900
form right where it sits in the list filled in with the current title and body.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:40.380
There is a save button and a cancel button.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.860
Where do those current values come from?

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.700
Each list item carries them in two data attributes, data title and data body set when the page

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.140
renders and updated after every save.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:57.340
So the form always starts from what is actually on the page instead of trying to read it back

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.440
out of the displayed text.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.240
Here is a detail that matters more than it looks.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:11.160
Each input in that form is created on its own and its value is set as plain text.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.360
It is not written into one long string of HTML.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.120
Why does that matter?

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.960
Because a title is whatever someone typed and people type double quotes.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:29.300
If you glue a title into a string of HTML, a title like he said, quote, high, quote,

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:34.380
ends the attribute at the first quote, the edit form opens showing only the words he

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.820
said and saving it quietly throws the rest away.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:44.540
A title written to close the tag can go further and run as code in the page.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:49.420
Setting the value as text avoids all of that quotes and all.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:53.620
The same rule applies when the saved entry goes back on the page.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.580
Its title and body are set as text content, never as HTML.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.760
If you remember one sentence from this section, make it this one.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.900
Never build HTML out of what someone typed.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.000
When you press save, the page sends a put request with fetch, carrying the new title

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.380
and body as JSON.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:20.460
The same 5 second limit from unit 08 applies, so a server that never answers ends in an

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:24.100
error instead of a page that waits forever.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:30.700
If the save works, the form is swapped back for the updated entry with no page reload.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:35.640
If it fails for any reason, the page says so, leaves the form open with your changes

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:39.780
still in it and lets you try again.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.180
Cancel is the simple one.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.780
It puts the original entry back and sends nothing to the server at all.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:52.140
So there are only two ways out of the edit form and only one of them talks to the server.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.660
Put carries one more idea worth having a word for.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:57.780
The word is EOIDEMPATENT.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:04.180
It means that sending the exact same request again leaves things in the same state as sending

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.380
it once.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:12.420
Send the same put five times and the entry ends up exactly as it would after one.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:16.580
Send the same post five times and you have five entries.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:18.500
That difference is not trivia.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.620
It decides what a client is allowed to do when something goes wrong.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.780
Picture a phone on a weak signal.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:29.900
It sends a request and the connection drops before the answer comes back.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.180
Did the server get it?

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.820
The phone cannot tell.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:37.780
If the request is idempotent, it can simply send it again because repeating it changes

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:39.140
nothing.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:43.900
If it is not, sending it again might create a second record so the client has to stop

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.820
and ask the person.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:52.980
That is why browsers and libraries will quietly retry some requests and never others.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:59.340
You might wonder why the edit form uses fetch at all when Unit 08 showed a classic form that

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.480
works with no JavaScript.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.000
The answer is a limit in HTML itself.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.940
An HTML form can only send get or post.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:14.680
If you write method equals put on a form, the browser does not complain.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:18.040
It quietly sends a get instead.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:22.040
Server rendered frameworks work around this with something called a method override.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:28.520
The form sends a post with a hidden field often named underscore method that says what

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.200
it really means.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.920
A small piece of middleware on the server reads that field and treats the request as

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.520
a put or a delete before the routes see it.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.160
You will not need it in this course, but you will see underscore method in other people's

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:47.920
code and now you know why it is there.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.020
There is one thing this version of the app gets wrong and the unit says so plainly.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:57.720
Right now an entry's id is just its position in the array.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:03.600
Delete an entry and every entry after it moves down one place.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.680
Inside one browser tab, the code renumbers the list after a delete so everything stays

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:09.960
in step.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.080
But a second tab does not know anything changed.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.080
An edit form left open there still holds the old position.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:23.540
Save it and it can land on whichever entry has moved into that spot and overwrite it.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:29.380
The home exercise has you handle the case the server can detect the entry your editing

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:35.300
is gone, the save comes back 404 and the page should remove that stale entry instead of

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.060
leaving a form with nowhere to go.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:44.880
The real fix comes in unit 12 when every entry gets an id from the database that never changes

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.380
when other entries are deleted.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.420
Here is what to keep from this unit.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.300
The crud is create, read, update and delete.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.940
In this app that is post, get, put and delete.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:04.180
Put replaces the whole entry at an id and answers 404 when there is no entry there and

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.860
400 when the new values fail validation.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:13.420
Idempotent means a repeat changes nothing so a repeated put is safe to retry and a repeated

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.880
post is not.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:19.820
One validation function guards every root that takes entry data and the edit form never

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.820
builds HTML out of what someone typed.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.180
Next is unit 11.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:27.860
Right now every route does everything itself.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:33.740
It reads the request, checks the rules and reads and writes the file all in one function.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.780
Unit 11 pulls those jobs apart into layers.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:41.740
The same structure real production apps use so that unit 12 can swap the file underneath

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:44.340
for a real database.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:49.340
The unit page has the full code, the exercise and the tutorial videos.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:50.340
See you in class.
